1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,465
A food packet says eight grams of
protein in every hundred grams.

2
00:00:04,465 --> 00:00:07,804
A blood report says how
much sugar is in your blood.

3
00:00:08,204 --> 00:00:09,947
Someone measured how much.

4
00:00:10,397 --> 00:00:12,774
Diya reads the label on a biscuit packet.

5
00:00:12,774 --> 00:00:14,577
How did they know the protein?

6
00:00:14,927 --> 00:00:16,401
Protein has nitrogen.

7
00:00:16,401 --> 00:00:20,083
Measure the nitrogen, and
you can work out the protein.

8
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:24,776
By the end, you will find the percentage
of each element in a compound.

9
00:00:25,426 --> 00:00:27,862
Last time we found what is inside.

10
00:00:27,862 --> 00:00:29,507
Now we find how much.

11
00:00:29,507 --> 00:00:31,553
This is quantitative analysis.

12
00:00:31,903 --> 00:00:33,925
The trick is always the same.

13
00:00:33,925 --> 00:00:38,588
Turn the element into a product we
can weigh, or a gas we can measure.

14
00:00:38,988 --> 00:00:43,427
Then the percentage is the mass of
the element, divided by the mass of

15
00:00:43,427 --> 00:00:45,651
the compound, times one hundred.

16
00:00:46,251 --> 00:00:48,287
Carbon and hydrogen come first.

17
00:00:48,287 --> 00:00:49,877
This is Liebig's method.

18
00:00:49,877 --> 00:00:52,175
Let's run it on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:57,454
Burn a known mass of the compound in
oxygen, over hot copper two oxide.

20
00:00:57,454 --> 00:00:59,523
Carbon becomes carbon dioxide.

21
00:00:59,523 --> 00:01:01,166
Hydrogen becomes water.

22
00:01:01,566 --> 00:01:05,645
The water is trapped in a tube
of anhydrous calcium chloride.

23
00:01:05,645 --> 00:01:09,549
The carbon dioxide is trapped
in strong potassium hydroxide.

24
00:01:09,949 --> 00:01:12,085
Weigh each tube before and after.

25
00:01:12,085 --> 00:01:14,324
The extra mass is what it trapped.

26
00:01:14,924 --> 00:01:15,824
Step one.

27
00:01:15,758 --> 00:01:19,887
Forty four grams of carbon dioxide
hold twelve grams of carbon.

28
00:01:20,637 --> 00:01:21,537
Step two.

29
00:01:21,453 --> 00:01:26,414
So percent carbon is twelve over forty
four, times the mass of carbon dioxide,

30
00:01:26,414 --> 00:01:29,636
times a hundred, over
the mass of the compound.

31
00:01:30,386 --> 00:01:31,286
Step three.

32
00:01:31,262 --> 00:01:34,445
Eighteen grams of water
hold two grams of hydrogen.

33
00:01:34,445 --> 00:01:37,038
So for hydrogen, use two over eighteen.

34
00:01:37,788 --> 00:01:39,663
Nitrogen has two methods.

35
00:01:39,663 --> 00:01:43,811
In Dumas method, the compound
is heated with copper oxide.

36
00:01:43,811 --> 00:01:46,635
Its nitrogen comes out as nitrogen gas.

37
00:01:47,035 --> 00:01:52,180
The gas passes through potassium hydroxide,
which soaks up the carbon dioxide.

38
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:57,335
We measure the volume of nitrogen left,
and change it to standard conditions.

39
00:01:57,735 --> 00:02:01,627
At standard conditions, twenty two
thousand four hundred millilitres of

40
00:02:01,627 --> 00:02:03,663
nitrogen weigh twenty eight grams.

41
00:02:04,263 --> 00:02:07,943
In Kjeldahl's method, the compound
is heated with strong sulphuric acid.

42
00:02:07,943 --> 00:02:09,960
Its nitrogen becomes ammonium sulphate.

43
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:12,976
Add alkali, and ammonia gas comes off.

44
00:02:12,976 --> 00:02:15,661
It is caught in a known amount of acid.

45
00:02:15,661 --> 00:02:18,287
The acid used up tells us the nitrogen.

46
00:02:18,687 --> 00:02:22,724
But Kjeldahl's method fails for nitro
and azo groups, and for nitrogen

47
00:02:22,724 --> 00:02:24,686
inside a ring, like pyridine.

48
00:02:25,286 --> 00:02:27,160
Halogens use the Carius method.

49
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:31,246
Heat the compound with fuming nitric
acid and silver nitrate, in a sealed

50
00:02:31,246 --> 00:02:32,146
glass tube.

51
00:02:32,467 --> 00:02:34,455
The halogen becomes silver halide.

52
00:02:34,455 --> 00:02:35,365
We weigh it.

53
00:02:35,365 --> 00:02:39,419
Silver bromide is one eighty eight
grams per mole, and eighty of that is

54
00:02:39,419 --> 00:02:40,319
bromine.

55
00:02:40,446 --> 00:02:43,970
Sulphur is heated the same way,
and becomes sulphuric acid.

56
00:02:43,970 --> 00:02:48,055
Barium chloride turns it into barium
sulphate, two thirty three grams per

57
00:02:48,055 --> 00:02:48,955
mole.

58
00:02:48,961 --> 00:02:52,492
Phosphorus becomes phosphoric acid, and
is weighed as ammonium phosphomolybdate.

59
00:02:52,892 --> 00:02:55,305
Oxygen is usually found by difference.

60
00:02:55,305 --> 00:02:59,757
Add up all the other percentages,
and take them away from one hundred.

61
00:03:00,357 --> 00:03:02,664
Compare the two nitrogen methods.

62
00:03:02,664 --> 00:03:05,947
Dumas works for every
kind of nitrogen compound.

63
00:03:06,347 --> 00:03:10,421
Kjeldahl is quick and simple, but it
misses nitro, azo and ring nitrogen.

64
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:12,091
Pause and think.

65
00:03:12,091 --> 00:03:14,051
A compound has a nitro group.

66
00:03:14,051 --> 00:03:16,096
Which method finds its nitrogen?

67
00:03:19,296 --> 00:03:20,196
Dumas.

68
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:23,073
Kjeldahl cannot turn nitro
nitrogen into ammonium sulphate.

69
00:03:23,723 --> 00:03:24,811
Remember this.

70
00:03:24,811 --> 00:03:29,356
Mass of the element, over mass of
the compound, times one hundred.

71
00:03:30,056 --> 00:03:31,937
Now, one worked example.

72
00:03:31,937 --> 00:03:33,818
These are example figures.

73
00:03:34,118 --> 00:03:38,801
Point two four six grams of a compound
give point one nine eight grams

74
00:03:38,801 --> 00:03:43,250
of carbon dioxide, and point one
zero one four grams of water.

75
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:44,650
Carbon.

76
00:03:44,392 --> 00:03:49,044
Twelve over forty four, times point
one nine eight, times a hundred, over

77
00:03:49,044 --> 00:03:50,446
point two four six.

78
00:03:50,446 --> 00:03:53,248
That is twenty one
point nine five percent.

79
00:03:53,998 --> 00:03:54,898
Hydrogen.

80
00:03:54,750 --> 00:03:59,376
Two over eighteen, times point one
zero one four, times a hundred, over

81
00:03:59,376 --> 00:04:00,799
point two four six.

82
00:04:00,799 --> 00:04:03,339
That is four point five eight percent.

83
00:04:04,089 --> 00:04:07,309
The rest is other elements,
found by their own tests.

84
00:04:07,959 --> 00:04:09,420
Now, the exam trap.

85
00:04:09,770 --> 00:04:12,135
For hydrogen, students
write one over eighteen.

86
00:04:12,535 --> 00:04:14,943
But water has two hydrogen atoms.

87
00:04:14,943 --> 00:04:16,637
Use two over eighteen.

88
00:04:17,287 --> 00:04:18,638
Where is this asked?

89
00:04:18,638 --> 00:04:22,756
JEE Main gives the masses and asks
for a percentage, often by Carius or

90
00:04:22,756 --> 00:04:23,656
Kjeldahl.

91
00:04:23,445 --> 00:04:26,012
Boards ask for the method and the formula.

92
00:04:26,612 --> 00:04:27,784
Exam level now.

93
00:04:27,784 --> 00:04:32,343
In a Carius method, point three zero
zero grams of a compound give point

94
00:04:32,343 --> 00:04:34,896
two eight two grams of silver bromide.

95
00:04:34,896 --> 00:04:37,190
What is the percentage of bromine?

96
00:04:37,190 --> 00:04:38,412
Pause and solve.

97
00:04:43,812 --> 00:04:45,228
The answer is A.

98
00:04:45,228 --> 00:04:46,402
Forty percent.

99
00:04:46,902 --> 00:04:50,479
Bromine is eighty over one
eighty eight of silver bromide.

100
00:04:50,979 --> 00:04:55,160
Eighty, times point two eight two,
times a hundred, over one eighty eight

101
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:56,324
times point three.

102
00:04:56,824 --> 00:04:58,275
That is forty percent.

103
00:04:58,275 --> 00:05:00,857
B forgot the eighty over one eighty eight.

104
00:05:01,457 --> 00:05:02,997
Here is your recap.

105
00:05:02,997 --> 00:05:04,580
Screenshot this card.

106
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:09,966
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:05:10,366 --> 00:05:11,266
One.

108
00:05:10,822 --> 00:05:14,264
Which tubes trap water and carbon
dioxide in Liebig's method?

109
00:05:15,064 --> 00:05:15,964
Two.

110
00:05:15,455 --> 00:05:18,050
Why does Kjeldahl's method
fail for a nitro compound?

111
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:19,750
Three.

112
00:05:19,453 --> 00:05:21,381
How is oxygen usually found?

113
00:05:22,281 --> 00:05:26,728
Next time: what do cooking gas,
petrol and candle wax have in common?

114
00:05:26,728 --> 00:05:28,344
We find out in alkanes.
